  <p class="lede first reveal">Most Shopify stores that never launch die in the same place: the trial. Someone opens an account on a Tuesday with a vague idea, spends nine evenings choosing fonts, and the store expires with no product in it. The tool wasn't the problem. The order of operations was.</p>
  <p class="reveal">So this is a plan with a clock on it. Friday is for deciding, Saturday for building, Sunday for the boring pages and one real order placed by you. The order matters more than the speed: product first, store second, fulfilment app third, buyers last. Which is why the theme comes nowhere near Friday night.</p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="friday">Friday night: decide before you open Shopify</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Three decisions get made on Friday, and none of them happen inside Shopify: what the one product is, what the store is called, and which supplier app will make and ship it.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Do not open a Shopify account tonight. The trial is short and the $1 months count from the day you start. Friday is a notes app and about ninety minutes.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The product.</strong> One. Not a range. A single item you can describe in one sentence to one kind of woman: <em>a heavyweight tote for nurses who carry their whole shift in one bag.</em> If you're still torn between three ideas, that's a different weekend's work. Tonight you need a decision you can live with for a month, not a perfect one.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The name.</strong> Short, easy to say out loud, not tied to the single product. Check the domain is available before you fall in love with it. Twenty minutes, maximum.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The supplier app.</strong> If your product is printed — apparel, mugs, totes, posters — you're starting with print on demand, and Printful, Printify or Gelato are the sensible first choices; all three are free to install. If it's an existing item someone else stocks, you're dropshipping, and you only use a supplier with warehouses in your own country. The US de minimis duty exemption ended in 2025, so China-direct parcels now arrive with duties, brokerage fees and delays that your customer will blame on you.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Write all three on one line. Product, name, app. That's Saturday's brief. Close the laptop.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Nobody has ever failed to sell because the theme was wrong. Plenty have failed because they spent the trial on the theme.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/print-on-demand-vs-dropshipping">Print on Demand vs Dropshipping: Which to Start With →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="saturday-am">Saturday morning: opening the store</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Open the account, pick a free theme, and set the six settings that decide whether an order can actually go through. That's the whole morning, and it's about two hours.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Now you open Shopify. New stores get a 3-day free trial and then <a href="https://shopify.pxf.io/eK3xYz" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">Shopify's $1-a-month start</a> (affiliate link) for the first three months on monthly billing, before the Basic plan's usual price kicks in. Those are Shopify's prices and they change, so read the screen rather than trusting me. Sign up with Friday's name, choose Basic, and ignore every upsell on the way in.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>The theme.</strong> Filter the theme store to free and pick the one whose demo looks closest to a single-product shop. Install it, and that's it. You can change it any time without losing a product.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Then the six settings that matter today.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Store name and contact email.</strong> Under store details. This is the address your order emails come from, so make it one you check.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Currency.</strong> The currency your customers think in. Change it now; changing it after your product is priced is a headache.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Shipping zone.</strong> One zone, your own country, one rate: flat, or free with the cost built into the price. Add international later, once you've seen what your supplier actually charges.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Taxes toggle.</strong> Tell Shopify whether to collect sales tax or VAT for your country, and whether your prices include it. If you're unsure of your own obligations, that's a question for an accountant, not a Sunday.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Shopify Payments.</strong> Turn it on and complete the identity and bank details. On Basic it takes 2.9% plus 30¢ per online card payment, and it's the difference between a store that looks live and one that can be paid. Do it before lunch; verification can take a while.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Legal pages.</strong> Under policies, generate the privacy, terms, refund and shipping templates. You'll rewrite shipping and returns on Sunday, but the placeholders need to exist for checkout to behave.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> The six that matter</span><p>Store name and email · Currency · One shipping zone · Taxes toggle · Shopify Payments on and verified · Legal pages generated. Everything else in settings can wait until you have a customer.</p></div>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="saturday-pm">Saturday afternoon: the app and the first product</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Install the supplier app you chose on Friday, push one product into the store, then write the product page like a person and not a catalogue.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Go to the app store inside Shopify, find the app on your Friday line, and install it. Every one of these apps then walks you through the same shape: choose a blank, upload your design or pick the stocked item, choose colours and sizes, set a retail price, push it to Shopify. Push one. The temptation to push twelve colourways is how a two-hour job becomes a week.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Before you leave the app, check the design is yours. Print on demand apps remove anything using a cartoon character, a sports team logo, a band name, a meme or a font licensed for personal use only, and recolouring it does not make it safe.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Now open the product in Shopify and fix the page the app generated, because it will read like a spec sheet. It needs four things.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A title in plain words.</strong> What it is and who it's for. <em>The Twelve-Hour Tote for Night Shift</em> beats <em>Heavyweight Canvas Tote Bag 15oz</em>. Specs go in the description, not the name.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Three benefits, not ten features.</strong> The three reasons Friday's woman would want it, in sentences she'd say herself. Fits a lunchbox and a laptop. Straps that don't dig in. Materials and dimensions underneath, for the people who scroll for them.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Sizing and shipping honesty.</strong> Say how long it takes, including the production days your app quotes, and put the supplier's size chart on the page. Print on demand is made to order, so there are no returns for a wrong size or a change of heart; you pay for the reprint unless the item is faulty. A clear size chart is cheaper than paying for her guess.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Mockups that look like a photo.</strong> Pick the two or three that look most like the item in use, not the flat product on a white square. Real photos come after your sample arrives.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Write the product page for one specific woman and read it back as her. If a sentence makes you wince, it's a feature dressed up as a benefit.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/shopify-supplier-apps">The Supplier Apps That Plug Into Shopify, Compared →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="sunday">Sunday: the pages people check before they trust you</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Before a stranger pays a store she's never heard of, she checks four things: who's behind it, what happens if it goes wrong, whether she can reach a human, and whether the web address looks real. Sunday builds those four.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>An about page without a face.</strong> You don't need a photo of yourself. You need a reason. Three short paragraphs: why this product exists, who it's for, and how it's made and shipped. What people are checking for is whether a real person with a point of view made this, and that comes through in the words, not the headshot.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A shipping and returns page in plain English.</strong> Rewrite the template. Production plus delivery time, as a range. What you do about a damaged or misprinted item (replace it, free, with a photo). What you can't do (exchange a size on a made-to-order item). Honest is more trusted than generous here; nobody believes a brand-new store offering ninety-day returns on custom goods.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A contact page.</strong> Shopify's contact form, plus Saturday's email. It's not for people to write to you; it's for people to see that they could.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>A real domain.</strong> Buy the one you checked on Friday and connect it under domains in settings. The free myshopify address is a small, silent signal that a store is temporary, and a real domain costs less than lunch.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Put all four in the footer, and about and contact in the main menu next to your one product. That's the whole navigation.</p>
  <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> Sunday's four</span><p>About (no face required) · Shipping and returns, rewritten honestly · Contact form and email · Real domain connected. All four in the footer, about and contact in the main menu.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/branding-guide">The Branding Guide →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter  reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="test">The test order</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Place a real order on your own store, let it run all the way to a parcel on your doorstep, and refund yourself. It's the only way to know the store works before a stranger finds out for you.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Sunday afternoon, open your store in a private browser window so you see it as a stranger does. Read the page, add to cart, check out with a real card, pay. Don't use Shopify's test mode. Test mode tells you the checkout works; a real order tells you the <em>supplier</em> works, and that's the part you can't see from the dashboard.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Then watch. Within minutes you should have a confirmation email in the customer inbox and a new-order notification in yours. Open the supplier app and confirm the order is sitting there. If it isn't, the connection is broken and you've found out for a dollar's worth of stress instead of a customer's.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Now wait for the parcel. This is your sample, and it does two jobs: it shows you whether the print, the sizing and the packaging are what you'd be proud to send, and it gives you real photos to replace the mockups. If it disappoints, change the print provider (Printify lets you switch providers for the same product) or the blank, and order again. Never sell something you haven't held.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Finally, refund the order in Shopify so your books are clean. The supplier's charge stays; that's the correct cost of a sample.</p>
  <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Everything in the store looks finished from the inside. The test order is the only view from the outside, and the outside is the only view that ever pays you.</p></div>
  <p class="deeper reveal"><span>Go deeper —</span> <a href="/learn/first-10-shopify-sales">How to Get Your First 10 Shopify Sales Without Ads →</a></p>

  <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">06</span><div class="num">Chapter Six</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="not">What not to do this weekend</h2></div>
  <p class="answer reveal">Don't add forty products, don't buy a paid theme, don't run ads, and don't spend a week on a logo. Each one feels like progress and each one is a way of avoiding the first sale.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Don't add forty products.</strong> One product with a clear reason for it converts better than forty with no point of view. Every extra product is another page to write and another size chart to get wrong. Add the second when the first has sold.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Don't buy a paid theme.</strong> You can't know what you'd want from one until you've watched real people use the store.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Don't run ads.</strong> Not this weekend, and not next weekend either. Ads pour strangers onto a page no stranger has tested, and charge you for every lesson the test order teaches for free. The first ten sales can come without paying for a click, and that's the next article in this run.</p>
  <p class="reveal"><strong>Don't build a logo for a week.</strong> Type the store name in a clean font. That's the logo for now. A real identity is worth doing, but it's a job for a store that has proven someone wants what's in it.</p>
  <p class="reveal">If you want the same build laid out lesson by lesson, that's what The Shopify Edit inside the Standard membership does ($9/month or $99/year, with a 7-day free trial), and The Shopify Starter Kit is the free download that goes with this run. But nothing in either replaces what you can do right now: one product, one app, one order, and a store that's live by Sunday evening.</p>
